%n ... ānah āšuš amṭī-ma
#note: The first verb of this enumeration is unclear in its etymology: @bib{RN2721@K 35a} treats it as belonging to the, otherwise unattested, verb @i{kâdu} B “to be distressed(?)”. @bib{RN262@272 ad iii 9} suggests to read it as @i{akkad}, derived from @i{nakādu} “to beat; to be frightened”. As she admits herself, this form should be @i{akkud}. The first-millennium version of the Cuthaean Legend (l. 88) has @akk{a-ka-la} in its place, likewise without a straightforward explanation.
